==English==

===Etymology===
Related to earlier {{m|en|hand over hand}}, this phrase originally referred to a speedy method of climbing up or hauling on a rope.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-hand over fist.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# ''{{lb|en|idiomatic}} Quickly or in great [[quantity]], especially in reference to earning [[money]].''
#: ''He didn't earn much, but the company seemed to rake in money '''hand over fist'''.''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1880|author=Robert Louis Stevenson|title=[[s:The Letters of Robert Louis Stevenson Volume 1|Letters vol. I]]|section=[[s:The Letters of Robert Louis Stevenson Volume 1/Chapter IV|chapter 4]]
|passage=Are you coming over again to see me some day soon? I keep returning, and now '''hand over fist''', from the realms of Hades: I saw that gentleman between the eyes, and fear him less after each visit.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1888|author=Rolf Boldrewood|title=[[s:Robbery Under Arms|Robbery Under Arms]]|section=[[s:Robbery Under Arms/Chapter 39|chapter 39]]
|passage=Now, since cattle had risen and meat and all to such a price, he was making money '''hand over fist'''.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|rapidly}} {{l|en|hand over hand}}

